WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.919
This video is sponsored by Skillshare.

00:00:02.919 --> 00:00:06.350
Learn something new with the link in the description.

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:10.769
There is perhaps no simpler toy than the humble
LEGO brick.

00:00:10.769 --> 00:00:16.960
The iconic four plain flat sides and two studs
are simple to make, simple to use, and simple

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.460
to learn.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.500
One would also think they’d be simple to
sell.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:27.079
And yet the LEGO Group has had a remarkable
number of ups and downs in its 88-year history

00:00:27.079 --> 00:00:31.890
— scares during which it seemed to face
certain death, and high points during which

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:33.950
it seemed invincible.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:39.660
The company’s path has been so turbulent
because, as it turns out, selling LEGO really

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.260
isn’t that simple.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:48.360
A mere 6 bricks can be combined in an astonishing
915 million different ways.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:52.940
The LEGO Group is tasked with finding which
of these combinations will excite children

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:57.039
the most and, ultimately, create the most
profit.

00:00:57.039 --> 00:01:02.629
And while the buildable brick may seem an
obvious invention today, they were once neither

00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:05.610
obvious nor easy to make.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:10.740
At a time when premium toys were all made
of wood, plastic LEGO bricks were a radical

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.280
departure from the norm.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:15.630
They seemed cheap, less friendly.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:19.610
The decision to use plastic was also far from
straightforward.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:24.020
Initially, bricks lacked the “sticking”
mechanism in which they stay together until

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:30.299
deliberately detached — meaning any creation
could be destroyed with one flick of a finger.

00:01:30.299 --> 00:01:35.630
After years of experimenting with different
materials, in 1958, LEGO settled on a design

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:41.040
that remains unchanged to this day — meaning
those 62-year-old bricks can still be stacked

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.829
with those manufactured yesterday.

00:01:43.829 --> 00:01:49.700
The second revolutionary idea of its founder
was called the “LEGO System of Play” — that

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:54.530
every set would be composed of the same basic
bricks — allowing them to be combined to

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:57.009
produce something even grander.

00:01:57.009 --> 00:02:02.610
The financial genius of this idea was simple:
Now, not only would LEGO have a brand, like

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:08.950
all companies, that consumers would grow to
love, but they would also invest in the ecosystem.

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:14.040
The interoperability meant that once a child
bought his or her first LEGO set, they now

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.920
lived in a LEGO household.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.250
There was more value to be had from buying
more and more sets.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:25.360
It was on these two foundational pillars that
LEGO grew to become the global sensation it

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:26.780
is today.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:33.630
As of 2013, a total of 4.4 billion Minifigures
have been produced, 80 LEGO bricks per person

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:40.960
on earth, and 300 million tires per year — technically
making it the largest producer of tires, above

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.130
Michelin and Goodyear.

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:45.110
But it wasn’t all smooth sailing.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:50.320
There were many missteps and challenges along
the way which directly led to the LEGO we

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:56.980
know today — one of movies and licensed,
branded themes like Star Wars and Minecraft.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:02.000
The first of these problems began to surface
at the end of the 1980s, when the last of

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:09.200
its core patents expired, ending its long-held
monopoly on interlocking, buildable toy blocks.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:13.820
In hindsight, although LEGO had seen decades
of profit, this was the first time it was

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:17.600
tested as a company, not merely as a product.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.090
During the life of the patent, LEGO could
coast on the brilliant idea and design of

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:26.570
its founder, knowing that no other company
could sell in the same market.

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:32.230
This insulated the company from competition,
creating a culture in which feedback or suggestions

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:36.370
from parents, or even children themselves,
was unwelcome.

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:40.850
Its designers took a similar philosophy to
those of Apple — that they could create

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:43.700
demand and that they didn’t need to chase
it.

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:47.230
In 1988, however, all that changed.

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:53.870
Now, any toy giant could sell similar brightly-colored,
themed playsets, which kids may or may not

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:55.990
be able to distinguish.

00:03:55.990 --> 00:04:02.260
At first, the LEGO Group shamelessly sued
its competitors — MEGA Bloks, Oxford Bricks,

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:07.030
and others — claiming that although its
patents had expired, their similar designs

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:10.200
were now infringing on its trademarks.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.820
When this failed — miserably — it took
a different approach.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:19.100
It decided to throw every theme, set, and
character out there and just see what stuck.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:25.960
Between 1994-98, LEGO tripled the number of
toys it produced — introducing at least

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:28.650
5 new themes every single year.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:33.270
The one new addition which succeeded — did
so fabulously.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:38.150
LEGO Bionicles had very little in common with
its counterparts — they were based on a

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:43.990
story of warring robots, were visually dark,
and used entirely different parts — but

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:46.180
kids loved them.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:52.640
Just a few years later, 85% of American boys
aged 6-12 said they knew of the brand, and

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.700
45% owned at least one of them.

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:59.060
Over 40 books were written based on the characters.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:05.180
Bionicles almost single-handedly kept the
company afloat, but was just one success atop

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.700
a mountain of failures.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:12.670
For example, there was Jack Stone — a more
detailed character based on a new series of

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:13.670
cartoons.

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:19.130
And Galidor: Defenders of the Outer Dimension
— a live-action sci-fi series which cost

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:22.860
LEGO millions and... almost no one watched.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:28.070
By 2003 it had become abundantly clear something
needed to change.

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:34.400
The company was on the edge of bankruptcy
with $800 million in debt, a 30% decline in

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:40.880
sales, and an estimated loss of $337,000 US
dollars every day.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:44.520
Even worse, the company didn’t really know
why.

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:50.900
This became the task of its then newly-hired
CEO in 2004, to figure out why the company

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:53.640
was losing so much money.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:58.500
Its original hypothesis was that kids were
increasingly turning towards modern alternatives

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:01.800
— video games, and electronic toys.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:04.180
This turned out to be wrong.

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:08.060
The real problem, it discovered, came from
within.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:10.820
The economics of LEGO are simple:

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:16.200
What makes it such a compelling toy is that
every set is composed of the same basic pieces,

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:19.540
which can be creatively rearranged and built-upon.

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:24.660
This just happens to also be what makes it
such a fantastic business.

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:29.880
A single, modular, universal part which can
be manufactured and later assembled into whatever

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:35.850
final product is in demand at that time, is
a supply chain manager’s dream.

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:42.139
Producing a given LEGO piece requires a $50-80,000
plastic injection mold.

00:06:42.139 --> 00:06:48.360
A popular brick may be produced 60 million
times — driving that mold cost down to essentially

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:49.500
zero.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:56.060
However, in LEGO’s decade of aimless experimentation
of new sets and themes, the number of unique

00:06:56.060 --> 00:07:00.870
pieces doubled — from 6,000 to over 12,000.

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:05.330
The company’s designers, insulated from
the financial costs of their decisions, had

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:09.130
been allowed to go crazy with new, exciting
themes.

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:15.140
One set, for example, included 8 different
pirates, with 10 different styles of legs.

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:20.510
Each of those new pieces required its own
mold, and thus, rapidly increased production

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:21.510
costs.

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:26.550
LEGO’s new CEO started by consolidating
— getting rid of unprofitable sets, and

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:29.139
doubling down on those that worked.

00:07:29.139 --> 00:07:34.620
What he found was that successful sets generally
had one thing in common: they centered around

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:39.200
a narrative, especially a branded series,
like a movie.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:43.450
Although partnering with existing brands like
Star Wars was initially controversial within

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:47.550
the company — they were worried it would
dilute its “family-friendly”, squeaky-clean

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:50.730
image — they proved to be immensely popular.

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:57.810
In 2004, only Star Wars and Bionicles sets
were making a profit, while the other 94%

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:58.900
lost money.

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:04.010
The issue was that Star Wars sets only sold
well after the release of a new movie — making

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:08.850
LEGO sales highly dependent on the cinematic
schedule of its partner.

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:13.070
These factors combined to usher in the modern
era of LEGO.

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:17.690
To differentiate itself and diversify, it
strikes licensing deals with a large number

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:21.630
of brands, and increasingly, seeks to create
its own.

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:26.820
The success of the LEGO Movie allowed the
company to control its own branding, and keep

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:30.060
the story alive in the minds of younger generations.

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:32.990
Still, these decisions are controversial.

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:38.080
LEGO has long attracted a large adult following,
some of whom say the company has lost its

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:42.140
creative beginnings in favor of Hollywood
storytelling.

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:48.170
Some vocal adult fans say the LEGO of today
places too much emphasis on play and not enough

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:49.890
on building.

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:54.360
What no one can say is that the company hasn’t
stayed focused on quality.

00:08:54.360 --> 00:09:02.130
88 years ago, LEGO began as a creator of premium
toys, and today that’s no less true.

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:07.460
With or without a patent, customers choose
LEGO simply because, they’re better.

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:13.220
If anything, the premium-feel of the humble
LEGO brick has only grown — as you discover

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:18.890
those from your childhood still smoothly,
satisfyingly, click-in-place after decades

00:09:18.890 --> 00:09:20.540
of waiting.

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:24.970
If you’re someone who likes playing with
LEGO, there’s a pretty good chance you’re

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:29.330
creative and like learning new things — which
